---
import ContentLayout from '../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.ts';
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="The limits of what the calculators on this site can be relied on for, and why you should verify results before acting on them."
  path="/disclaimer"
  h1="Disclaimer"
  updated="13 September 2026"
  intro="What these calculators are for, and — more importantly — what they are not for."
>
  <div class="prose-site">
    <h2>General information only</h2>
    <p>
      Everything on {SITE.name} is provided for general informational and educational purposes. The
      calculators perform standard arithmetic on the numbers you enter. They do not know your
      circumstances and cannot judge whether the calculation you have chosen is the right one for your
      situation.
    </p>

    <h2>Not professional advice</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing on this site constitutes financial, investment, tax, accounting, legal, medical or
      academic advice. A percentage is a neutral piece of arithmetic; deciding what to do about it is
      not. For decisions that matter, consult a qualified professional who can take your full
      circumstances into account.
    </p>

    <h2>Accuracy</h2>
    <p>
      The calculation logic is covered by an automated test suite spanning whole numbers, decimals,
      negative values, very large numbers, zero, division by zero, floating-point precision and
      rounding. Even so, no software is guaranteed free of defects, and no warranty is given that
      every result is correct or fit for a particular purpose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two practical limits are worth naming. First, calculations use standard double-precision
      floating-point arithmetic, which is what every browser and spreadsheet uses; results are cleaned
      up to remove its familiar artefacts, but extremely large or extremely small values can still
      lose precision. Second, displayed answers are rounded — where a result is rounded it is marked
      with ≈, and you should not treat a displayed figure as exact to unlimited decimal places.
    </p>

    <h2>Verify before you act</h2>
    <p>
      Please check any figure independently before using it for something consequential — a
      contract, a tax return, an academic submission, a financial commitment or a medical dosage.
      Every calculator shows its formula and full working precisely so that you can check it.
    </p>

    <h2>Rounding and real-world rules</h2>
    <p>
      Real-world percentage calculations often follow conventions this site does not apply: tax
      authorities specify particular rounding rules, retailers round prices in particular ways, and
      institutions weight exam marks according to their own schemes. The arithmetic here is the
      standard mathematical calculation and may differ from an official figure for those reasons.
    </p>

    <h2>No liability</h2>
    <p>
      To the fullest extent permitted by law, no liability is accepted for any loss or damage
      resulting from use of this site or reliance on its results. Use of the site is at your own risk.
      See the <a href="/terms">terms of service</a> for the full position.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
    <p>
      If a result looks wrong, please report it to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>{' '}
      with the calculator, the numbers you entered and the answer you expected. Accuracy reports are
      the first thing looked at.
    </p>
  </div>
</ContentLayout>
